@* Both, because the section is called Links and Buttons and the component does both: the bubble
   IS the control. Not a row of buttons under the thread — the reader presses the thing they
   read, which is why every Bubble variant styles `bubble-content:is(button,a):hover`.

   The quick replies are submit buttons in a form, so they post with no JavaScript taking part.
   The last one is an anchor, so it goes somewhere, opens in a tab on middle-click and shows its
   destination in the status bar — none of which a click handler gives you. *@
<form method="post" action="/chat/1/reply" up-target=".preview" up-disable
      class="flex w-full max-w-sm flex-col gap-8">
    <AntiforgeryToken />

    <Bubble Variant="muted">
        <BubbleContent>How can I help you today?</BubbleContent>
    </Bubble>

    <BubbleGroup>
        @foreach (var (value, label) in Replies)
        {
            <Bubble Variant="tinted" Align="end">
                <BubbleContent Type="submit" name="reply" value="@value">@label</BubbleContent>
            </Bubble>
        }
    </BubbleGroup>
</form>

@* OUTSIDE the form. Unpoly resolves a link's options from the nearest ancestor that carries
   them, so a link inside this form inherited its up-target=".preview" — it fetched the docs
   page, tried to extract a fragment that is not in it, and threw. The bubble looked dead and
   the console said `Cannot read properties of undefined`. A link is not part of the form
   anyway; it is the other thing this section is called after. *@
<Bubble Variant="tinted" Align="end" Class="w-full max-w-sm">
    <BubbleContent Href="/components/button">Read the button docs instead</BubbleContent>
</Bubble>

@code {
    static readonly (string Value, string Label)[] Replies =
    [
        ("password", "I forgot my password"),
        ("subscription", "I need help with my subscription"),
        ("human", "Something else. Talk to a human."),
    ];
}
